  "narrative": "The new conditional analyses strongly reinforce the accepted TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss weaker-dependency associations. H39 and H40 show that TP53 loss remains associated with weaker composite dependency both when SMARCA4 is intact and when SMARCA4 is lost, with signed lower bounds of +0.346 and +0.206, both above the 0.15 threshold. H47 and H48 similarly show TP53-loss effects remain within KEAP1-intact and KEAP1-loss states, with lower bounds +0.265 and +0.414. Thus the accepted H13 association is not apparently carried merely by uneven distribution of SMARCA4 loss or KEAP1 loss. H41, H42, H49, and H50 provide the analogous checks for KEAP1 loss: the signed estimates are large, and all lower bounds are far above the 0.15 threshold. I accept these eight conditional mean-difference tests as robustness checks and close them; they should not be treated as eight separate new predictors, but as evidence that H13 and H14 are robust in co-alteration-stratified comparisons. I close H14 because its main and conditional evidence are now very consistent. H13 remains active because I request independent validation of the moderate TP53-loss claim.\n\nThe technical comparisons do not support meaningful positive associations. H27, testing Avana versus non-Avana CRISPR library background, has a signed estimate of +0.052 with a 95% interval from -0.038 to +0.143; the entire interval lies below the 0.15 meaningful threshold in the claimed direction. H28, testing matched RNA/DNA coverage, has a signed estimate of -0.030 and upper bound +0.050, also not supporting a meaningful positive association. Both are rejected and closed as useful negative technical diagnostics.\n\nCDKN2A loss within TP53-intact samples (H51) gives a signed estimate of +0.125, interval +0.051 to +0.199. This is directionally supportive of stronger composite dependency, but the point estimate is below the 0.15 threshold and the interval includes values on both sides of the threshold. I therefore keep it unresolved and defer it; the original H8 CDKN2A claim remains unresolved as well.\n\nPTEN loss as a weaker-dependency predictor is not supported as stated. H32 has signed estimate -0.227, interval -0.323 to -0.131. The sign indicates the opposite direction: PTEN-loss samples appear to have stronger composite dependency. I reject H32 as a weaker-dependency claim and register a new reversed-direction proposal for PTEN loss. The reversed absolute interval would be approximately +0.131 to +0.323, so the point estimate exceeds the 0.15 threshold but the lower bound does not clearly establish a meaningful effect; the reversed claim should be followed up rather than immediately accepted.\n\nFor independent validation, I request H13 rather than H14 because H13 has a moderate effect size, its non-Avana stratum remains imprecise, and independent confirmation would strengthen a key accepted association. H14 is already extremely consistent across the main comparison, library strata, SMARCA4-conditioned strata, and TP53-conditioned strata. All statements remain dataset-level descriptive associations, not causal or biologically generalized conclusions.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted central claims: H9 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger composite dependency, validated; H13 TP53 loss -> weaker composite dependency, accepted and active pending requested independent validation; H14 KEAP1 loss -> weaker composite dependency, accepted and closed after robustness checks across library, SMARCA4-conditioned, and TP53-conditioned strata. H8 CDKN2A loss remains unresolved: global and TP53-intact estimates are directionally supportive but below/near threshold with intervals crossing 0.15. New conditional robustness checks H39-H42 and H47-H50 are accepted and closed as support for H13/H14, not as separate predictors. Technical diagnostics H27 and H28 are rejected and closed: Avana library and matched RNA/DNA status do not show meaningful positive associations. H51 is deferred as CDKN2A within TP53-intact remains near threshold. H32 as PTEN weaker-dependency is rejected because observed direction is opposite; a new PTEN-loss stronger-dependency proposal has been registered with direction -1. The reversed PTEN interval is promising but not decisive because the implied lower bound is below threshold. Next analysis priorities: follow up the PTEN reversed-direction proposal; if capacity allows, add conditional PTEN tests within SMARCA4, TP53, and KEAP1 strata to assess confounding by the large central effects; evaluate subtype robustness H29-H31 or remaining genomic predictors H33-H38; defer or selectively test formal interactions H43-H46 unless needed. Do not accept H8 as meaningful without clearer threshold evidence, and do not interpret accepted associations as causal.",
